{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 42, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reclassifies small-scale possession as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth can face up to six months in jail—or opt for a behavioral health intervention paired with a diversion pathway to avoid penalties. The law also tightens restrictions on sales near areas like playgrounds and increases enforcement in public spaces.\n\n***\n\nPolice are now tasked with determining whether someone qualifies for a health-based alternative. Eligibility hinges on strict conditions: no outstanding warrants, no violent conduct, no other criminal charges, and physical stability. If criteria are met, officers contact a regional hotline to alert a mobile health team, which must arrive within half an hour. If they don’t, arrest becomes the default action—no delays permitted.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue the law gives local areas flexibility to tailor responses based on their capacity, while critics warn of inconsistent outcomes across regions. The absence of a statewide mandate for treatment services raises concerns about unequal access, especially in under-resourced areas. Law enforcement, while equipped with new protocols, remains bound by time-sensitive procedures that may not always align with the needs of those struggling.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reckoning over how to balance public safety and care. In many parts of the country, the path forward remains uncertain. Some wonder how a person’s age or life circumstances might influence their chances of receiving help instead of punishment. Others point out that even basic needs—like where to eat or how to shop—can feel out of reach when stigma and legal risk hang over every step. The tongue of policy, after all, often speaks louder than the hands that write it.\n\nP.P.S The age of reform is here, but the country still struggles to decide whether to punish or heal. To eat well, one needs stability. To shop safely, one needs trust. To speak without fear, one needs a tongue unchained by judgment.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.050031621009111404}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.9851841926574707}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.02703193575143814}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.9216428995132446}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.1843518614768982}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -6.5205356804654e-05}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.5231673121452332}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003511289251036942}, {"token": " misdemeanor", "logprob": -0.37775421142578125}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0006887924391776323}, {"token": " Under", "logprob": -0.1870725005865097}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -0.1758732944726944}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token"